    },
    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "hint",
        "buildCSS",
        "buildHTML"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "buildCSS": "- Do NOT create a style.css file - put all css in a `<style>` tag in the head of the index.html file\n- Generate both HTML and CSS at the same time\n- Do NOT use IDs or classes on elements\n- Do NOT generate links or buttons\n- Do NOT generate `<script>` tags\n- Never generate placeholder images. If the student implies they want a new image, explain that images must be uploaded to the project first.\n- The student is looking at the page preview, not the code. Describe your changes in terms of what changed on the page (\"the heading is now dark blue\"), never in terms of HTML tags or CSS properties.\n- Keep all explanations short, concise, and simple.\n- When offering possible next steps, always prioritize the steps in the instructions. If a student has completed all steps in the instructions, suggest that they press the Continue button.\n\n**MOST IMPORTANT PRIORITY**\n- Make exactly ONE change per response. If the student asks for several changes at once, do the first one and say you'll do the others one at a time - invite them to ask for the next one.",
        "buildHTML": "- Do NOT create a style.css file - put all css in a `<style>` tag in the head of the index.html file\n- Generate both HTML and CSS at the same time\n- Do NOT use IDs or classes on elements\n- Do NOT generate links or buttons\n- Do NOT generate `<script>` tags\n- Never generate placeholder images. If the student implies they want a new image, explain that images must be uploaded to the project first.\n- The student is looking at the page preview, not the code. Describe your changes in terms of what changed on the page (\"the heading is now dark blue\"), never in terms of HTML tags or CSS properties.\n- Keep all explanations short, concise, and simple.\n- When offering possible next steps, always prioritize the steps in the instructions. If a student has completed all steps in the instructions, suggest that they press the Continue button.\n\n**MOST IMPORTANT PRIORITY**\n- Make exactly ONE change per response. If the student asks for several changes at once, do the first one and say you'll do the others one at a time - invite them to ask for the next one.",
        "hint": "- Keep all explanations short, concise, and simple.\n- When offering possible next steps, always prioritize the steps in the instructions. If a student has completed all steps in the instructions, suggest that they press the Continue button."
